### Request 10: Should succeed (0 remaining)
GET http://localhost:5000/games
Accept: application/json

###

### Request 11: Should FAIL with 429 Too Many Requests
GET http://localhost:5000/games
Accept: application/json

# Expected response:
# HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests
# X-RateLimit-Limit: 10
# X-RateLimit-Remaining: 0
# X-RateLimit-Reset: <unix-timestamp>
# Retry-After: <seconds>
#
# {
#   "error": "Rate limit exceeded",
#   "message": "Too many requests. Limit: 10 requests per 60 seconds.",
#   "retryAfter": 45.2,
#   "limit": 10,
#   "windowSeconds": 60
# }

###

### Request 12: Also should FAIL with 429
GET http://localhost:5000/games
Accept: application/json

###

### Test different endpoint (tracked separately)
GET http://localhost:5000/games/1
Accept: application/json

# This endpoint has its own counter
# Should succeed even if /games is rate limited

###

### POST request (also tracked separately)
POST http://localhost:5000/games
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Rate Limit Test Game",
  "genre": "Testing",
  "description": "Testing rate limits",
  "releaseDate": "2025-12-28"
}

###

### Test after waiting 60 seconds
# Wait 60 seconds, then run this:
GET http://localhost:5000/games
Accept: application/json

# Should succeed - window has reset
# X-RateLimit-Remaining: 9

###

### Load test with cURL (run in terminal)
# for i in {1..15}; do curl -X GET http://localhost:5000/games -w "\nStatus: %{http_code}\n"; done

###

### Tip: Monitor Redis keys in real-time
# redis-cli KEYS "ratelimit:*"
# redis-cli ZRANGE "ratelimit:127.0.0.1" 0 -1 WITHSCORES
# redis-cli ZCARD "ratelimit:127.0.0.1"
# redis-cli TTL "ratelimit:127.0.0.1"
